  card="Alloying is a very good method of improving the properties of a metal. Iron is the most widely used metal but is never used in its pure state, because pure iron is very soft and stretches easily when hot. If it is mixed with a small amount of carbon (about 0.05 %), it becomes hard and strong, and when iron is mixed with nickel and chromium we get stainless steel, which is hard and does not rust. An alloy is a homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal, prepared by first melting the primary metal and then dissolving the other elements in it in definite proportions, then cooling. If one of the metals is mercury, the alloy is an amalgam. The electrical conductivity and melting point of an alloy is less than that of pure metals: brass and bronze are not good conductors, and solder, an alloy of lead and tin, has a low melting point and is used to weld electrical wires. Pure 24 carat gold is very soft, so it is alloyed with silver or copper; 22 carat gold has 22 parts pure gold to 2 parts of copper or silver.",
